{"title":"2026 USAJMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#(Actual)_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">(Actual) Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Fixed,_Cyclic_Orientation_and_Number_Theory)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Fixed, Cyclic Orientation and Number Theory)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> foxes and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> bunnies sitting in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> seats around a circular table, where all animals are distinguishable. If a fox and a bunny are sitting next to each other, they may swap positions with each other. For a fixed starting configuration, determine the number of configurations that can be reached by a sequence of swaps.\n</p><p>Note: Rotations and reflections of a configuration are considered distinct.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\".28Actual.29_Solution_1\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"(Actual)_Solution_1\">(Actual) Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: (Actual) Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The trivial cases of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e275495e7e44d279fb3e5051b3a63115e79ead5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/02349576cee613512cdf301a365f06c0760acab5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> break with the pattern, so we do not consider them (the answer here is trivially <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />). From now on, assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/1/341b90c8a460e5b6d5833a4f35301e18acd068de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nFix a fox, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Call the bunnies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/6/9b6c5146cc515893d8f1e52e11436953bbfe3966.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_1,B_2,\\ldots,B_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"113\" height=\"16\" /> in clockwise order with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/3/243af6fad28c15abb3e11996374ba60e745bf543.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> being the first bunny after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> in clockwise order. While we perform swaps, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> denote the index of the first bunny after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> in clockwise order. (So if, at a certain time, the bunny <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/9/609b70ddcb48c71fc8607df68592e87be711e755.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> is the first bunny after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/f/ccf5d650726be49af3a306c54a2fd7dd1d628b48.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=8$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> at that moment.) Furthermore, at each point in time, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> denote the seat number of fox <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, where we number the seats clockwise from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> with seat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> being the initial position of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> foxes and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> bunnies are arranged in a line (NOT a circle), then there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/a/c8a35728edaa2f478904183e650c752276bd4ae4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{m+n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"79\" height=\"55\" /> configurations since the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> foxes are in a foxed order and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> bunnies are in a fixed order, so this boils down to choosing the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> positions of the foxes from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> total placements. (All of these configurations are reachable since by repeatedly swapping foxes to the left, we reach a situation where all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> foxes are to the left of all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> bunnies. But swaps are reversible, which proves reachability.)\n</p><p><br />\nFor each different value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67ba6e769bbcf7b5e65b2f06a77a3c7fd345abab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1,2,\\ldots,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"114\" height=\"17\" />, we have a different set of configurations since the position of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> relative to the bunnies is distinct. Fix some value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />; then, the remaining <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/e/96eec32de93b032b6d531a03aa5b346156b62a75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"46\" height=\"12\" /> foxes and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> bunnies form a line since we are fixing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, the number of configurations is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/a/ffab9b14c76a13cbd4e428f55060c841be7523a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{m+n-1}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"110\" height=\"55\" />. However, when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is different, then we also (trivially) have a distinct configuration. Therefore, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> denotes the number of distinct ordered pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> among all configurations, then our answer is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/3/6932042c4dfdef323ac7325a7eebc5afb139c134.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A\\cdot\\binom{m+n-1}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"137\" height=\"55\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nTo count the number of ordered pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" />, consider the starting position, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/5/915f1781caa8b3e0eddafe7a445019f06178da22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=y=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />. Every swap of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> clockwise increases both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> when taken modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> respectively (where the residue <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is represented by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />, respectively from now on) since we must swap with a bunny, changing the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. Similarly, every swap of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> counterclockwise decreases both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. If we let the net number of clockwise swaps of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> so far be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, then the ordered pair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/b/dbbf418a8c75ef26e1fcb237b5ad1fd51f4e0b6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)=(1+k,1+k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"173\" height=\"20\" /> (again with their respective moduli taken).\n</p><p><br />\nWe want the number of solutions to the system of moduli <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/e/64e833956e898d463fd4e6a3b4793baffa7f8980.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\equiv 1+k\\pmod{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"152\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/7/e47dc2d950b144994b859111de8e83dce970bc2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y\\equiv 1+k\\pmod{m+n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"189\" height=\"20\" /> across all integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. But this is periodic with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> with period <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/5/8656a8f7e3230d49ff1033b60de23379ed7f28ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{lcm}(m+n,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"110\" height=\"20\" />; this is also the minimal period since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/9/7b9c4fa880689d872a94c2f48025117cca4ea969.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=\\text{lcm}(m+n,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"144\" height=\"20\" /> is the minimum positive value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> for which the ordered pair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> repeats itself by definition of LCM. Therefore, our answer is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/a/93aab0f26848e8285df10f40266a34db504d5ffe.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{lcm}(m+n,n)\\cdot\\binom{m+n-1}{n}=\\frac{n(m+n)}{\\gcd(m+n,n)}\\cdot\\frac{(m+n-1)!}{n!\\cdot(m-1)!}=\\frac{mn}{\\gcd(m,n)}\\cdot\\frac{(m+n)!}{m!\\cdot n!}=\\boxed{\\text{lcm}(m,n)\\cdot\\binom{m+n}{m}}\\]\" width=\"842\" height=\"65\" />\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Eevee9406\">eevee9406</a>\n</p><p>Some notes by solution writer: This problem was extremely sillyable since it has an unusual answer. The common incorrect answer likely comes from not all ordered pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> being possible due to their dependence on each other when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/5/b25e2b4634a2c2c2d6ccc8f018248d9a2f50d9e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m,n)\\ne 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />. Testing small cases would indicate more complexity; the case that I personally considered during the contest that led to this realization was <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/3/503faa0a51dbefac3901997578337141a761bd0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=n=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Fixed.2C_Cyclic_Orientation_and_Number_Theory.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Fixed,_Cyclic_Orientation_and_Number_Theory)\">Solution 2 (Fixed, Cyclic Orientation and Number Theory)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Fixed, Cyclic Orientation and Number Theory)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/e/aae304d52fbdead77fdb5ac54ba20ee321bad43b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Preliminary}:$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Note that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/a/c8a35728edaa2f478904183e650c752276bd4ae4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{m+n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"79\" height=\"55\" /> total possible configurations. Without loss of generality, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/a/8da67e35e9c517fa132e6b7d8b548f3940e80d2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\ge n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Define an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />-circle to be a partial circle consisting of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> members. The two foxes at the ends of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />-circle have the potential to swap. Such an example of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />-circle<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/b/38b5f28385fd171d149ac0d708318afe123e510d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$:~z = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is shown below.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Usajmoq2p2.1.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9c/Usajmoq2p2.1.png/450px-Usajmoq2p2.1.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"450\" height=\"205\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9c/Usajmoq2p2.1.png/675px-Usajmoq2p2.1.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9c/Usajmoq2p2.1.png/900px-Usajmoq2p2.1.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/0/d503c70e629fb2f0318fd94ecdeba6a3fed1aff2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Lemma 1}:$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>The foxes arranged in an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />-circle will have a minimum of 2 swaps. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/d/d1de0fec6a594e1872e61c08d0c38fb9902f5cb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Proof of Lemma 1}:$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"164\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Trivial through definition of an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />-circle, hence desired.\n</p><p>Letting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> denote the invariant number of possible swaps, it is given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> satisfies the inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/7/c17d88e2fdeef66df0e37def936f39b773567278.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/1/241bd1c534a7682effdd888f6eae66df96dabba6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Lemma 2}:$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>The upper bound of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, and there are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/1/391fa498cd6e4934d161f36af3e2b08fce4bf0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" /> swaps possible given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/6/3e6aef22f13f127cd80169d67cb82de1513de248.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Proof of Lemma 2}:$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"164\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>We label the foxes as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/3/ea3e72603460f44d92ba2e830511c3ba51d2d7b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,\\dots,m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"17\" /> and the rabbits as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/8/838228ac8ee711eda2bdae0818393f87407dbc66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,\\dots,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"15\" />. Consider pairings (or swaps) between foxes and rabbits.\n</p><p>If every fox can be paired with every rabbit independently, then the total number of possible pairings is simply\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/3/a83598d2ae5cd7de84bd91bb252642d4210396c8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[mn.\\]\" width=\"30\" height=\"8\" />\n</p><p>Now suppose the system imposes a <b>cyclical non-totient structure</b>, meaning foxes are arranged in a cycle of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and rabbits are arranged in a cycle of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, and pairings are considered equivalent under a certain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/a/3da4b33bf8e2e58f04633701fd64582c5c073662.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd \\neq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> (In other words, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are not relatively prime)\n</p><p>In this case, we are no longer counting raw pairs but rotations under\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/9/0e90e8027d3f1e96af06291069f4aeb489bf1d59.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(i,j) \\rightarrow (i+1 \\pmod m,\\; j+1 \\pmod n).\\]\" width=\"345\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The idea is that through basic modular arithmetic, every single <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> will map to every single <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" /> (where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/7/f97c9ab67be9f3ab0a96a9698ad77c08d9bbcddf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; i,j &lt; m, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"117\" height=\"17\" />) uniquely if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/2/4a25761b45455550318df56e21f5fd426d5916c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(i,j) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" />. To prove that statement, the concept of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chinese_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Chinese Remainder Theorem\">Chinese Remainder Theorem</a> immediately results in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/0/150c08478d63c3d2f5cc0de4222ea9183673d7ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$mn$\" width=\"26\" height=\"8\" />. However, if this is not the case, each cycle maps onto one another by some primitive root, in which every cycle is shortened by one factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/f/e1f322abb8bc3b75a04ef23da4f23b05425b3a34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> from its parent. To prove this statement, imagine applying <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chinese_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Chinese Remainder Theorem\">Chinese Remainder Theorem</a> to two elements <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" />, whose elements satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/b/c1bc8e39b186e49e6c354fa2d2b84f21aa5cc970.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(i,j) \\neq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" />. Obviously, to obtain some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/5/48589d58704d7475ba6e0d1a7f3465b210d5433f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = \\frac{i}{x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"50\" height=\"39\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/91929283922e1e7b2fac278c22fb1e8143c9bafb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" /> so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/6/2068a9c417ed4b53554dea6c5ad787e64bc4a1cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(k,j) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" /> to apply <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chinese_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Chinese Remainder Theorem\">Chinese Remainder Theorem</a>, we need to find the minimal value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> in primitive modulo that satisfies this. The smallest plausible that is apart of both primitive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/f/aff296b8b99b0177de02242979c8ff34d68d0734.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pmod{i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"71\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/f/d2fcde90297b0946cc2156b0b88d1bc84511f693.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pmod{j}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"20\" /> is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/7/ec79de7e6b8c756a2bb07f77d7e48b0b7a6d91a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" />, and thus the cases for this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/9/56993885521674a4ff8de53f0b68eaba07ba9c45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{mn}{\\gcd(m,n)} = \\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"184\" height=\"39\" />.\n</p><p>As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/9/ab925051d1b47f16f8e6c22dc673734cf580b089.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\operatorname{lcm}(m,n) = mn$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"20\" /> holds for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/a/c7a7986370c73f39e8e4cc79eaa379bac4e14eec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m,n) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />, it follows for conveniency that every single case falls into \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/7/3b76f427681739360dc128f377f837f2d74a7ee3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)\\]\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>and the upper bound of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is indeed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>(Note: While my previous interpretation failed to identify the idea of cyclicity and when the greatest common divisors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are not 1, it does correctly hold the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the upper bound as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is not dependent on the cyclicity. The mistake I made when constructing my and incorrectly fixing the previous solution 2 was simply not accounting for when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> were not relatively prime, which explains why I made the mistake. For that, I would like to apologize).\n</p><p>The diagram below is a visual interpretation of the upper bound of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Usajmoq2p2.2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/b/b0/Usajmoq2p2.2.png/450px-Usajmoq2p2.2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"450\" height=\"256\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/b/b0/Usajmoq2p2.2.png/675px-Usajmoq2p2.2.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/b/b0/Usajmoq2p2.2.png/900px-Usajmoq2p2.2.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e25752fb799ada67109e8807727bffd156c23be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Claim}:$\" width=\"58\" height=\"11\" />\n</p><p>The problem statement, that is, \"rotations are distinct\", is negligible given the above criteria. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/1/231448f971a35cb344269f5d288373dd45c19f97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Proof of Claim}:$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"136\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>As we cyclically pair each fox and rabbit together, the rotations have already been accounted for, hence proved.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/3/533bc2cb6c9030374ef47ab0e1ee3feaa251264c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{USAJMO Q2}:$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"124\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Thus the total number of such configurations is just the number of possible configurations, each of which multiplied by the number of distinct swaps per that given section. There are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/1/391fa498cd6e4934d161f36af3e2b08fce4bf0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" /> distinct swaps through Lemma 2 and a total of  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/a/c8a35728edaa2f478904183e650c752276bd4ae4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{m+n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"79\" height=\"55\" /> given distinct cases. Rearrangement using commutative property derives the count\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/7/99767e2479f2ffe335b2d0e37e7675df8f8be2fc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\binom{m+n}{m} \\cdot \\operatorname{lcm}(m,n) ~~~~~~~~~ \\square\\]\" width=\"226\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We start by considering which seats can have foxes while not changing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/9/e09119b4508cf42e083dd910dc9f977e6e734800.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I(F),I(R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"85\" height=\"20\" />. The claim is that all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/9/1997e7ebef0b533520518b35e207832b76cad30d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(^{m+n}_m\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"49\" height=\"23\" /> different ways to arrange foxes are possible. Assume the smallest indexed fox not in the seat with its own index is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> who is currently in seat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" />. This implies that all seats <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/1/8b1a2cbcb6957b43416f06386fb6882a5ed47284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i,i+1...j-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"17\" />  are occupied by rabbits <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/8/4180a5e342b44ae0f4b137a136c2a8a331bef66f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,3...j-i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"97\" height=\"17\" />. So we swap fox <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> with rabbit <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/3/1233d0dcd103cc6ee1353e914244e3d66573a39e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> then with rabbit <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/5/b25cef4da10ebf03b70323f1819cf343f9b2deab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" /> so on until swapped with rabbit <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, returning it to seat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />. None of these swaps involves seat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> swapping seat 1 so the order is preserved. So by induction we can places all the foxes in their respective seats (call this state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />). This means from any initial configuration we can reach <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. So if we consider a random configuration <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> we can also reach <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. So if we reach <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> from the initial configuration then reverse all the moves needed to reach <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> we actually end up in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> from the initial configuration. So all configurations are possible. Next we consider all possible orders of foxes and rabbits. we start from\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e063c96269d2a7a0e123373ee48ffd8b0ca8fc7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I(F)=1,2,3...m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/4/524fe86151198d3b8c97bcb69c03fba4b9d43df8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I(R)=1,2,3...n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"133\" height=\"20\" />. The only way to change the order is to swap the first fox with the last rabbit resulting in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/8/a38e00704a52d4fe2e87d9561b4e7b58cabb6d6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I(F)=2,3...m,1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/f/74f71fc3e8043ae64ed7ada59870a583f19b5670.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I(R)=n,1,2,3...n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />. So each single change rotates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/9/e09119b4508cf42e083dd910dc9f977e6e734800.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I(F),I(R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"85\" height=\"20\" /> by 1 slot. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/9/da9862ffe331edea791e34270cedcc8719a45d7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I(F)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" /> rotates with period <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/3/8238e0dec492de46bddd71b7e2fe5ac346621f40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I(R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"20\" /> rotates with period <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. So the combined period of foxes and rabbits is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/1/391fa498cd6e4934d161f36af3e2b08fce4bf0f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" />. Combining the ways to seat the foxes and order the foxes &amp; rabbits, we get  \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb687a5046af788a50eeb58a0e30cf9f1a77c705.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\left(^{m+n}_m\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)\\]\" width=\"134\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>~WYC10041\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/69z4tSf1M0o\">https://youtu.be/69z4tSf1M0o</a> - Continuum Math\n</p><p>Note: The solution is incorrect but ~Continuum Math emphasized he will make an updated solution soon.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO\" title=\"2026 USAJMO\">2026 USAJMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems\" title=\"2026 USAJMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=176&amp;year=2026\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2026 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2026 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2026 USAJMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2026 USAJMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2026 USAJMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2026 USAJMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_USAJMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2026 USAJMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAJMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAJMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAJMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018022549\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.066 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.092 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 907/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1326/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 48/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 30096/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   17.600      1 -total\n 71.38%   12.563      1 Template:USAJMO_newbox\n 27.42%    4.825      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:36095:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018022549 and revision id 283523. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}